## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CV flow from detection to simple 3D pose using known object size, plus tracking and data strategy in a constrained, on-device setting.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular pose estimation with known dimensions
- Lightweight detectors (EfficientDet/YOLO-tiny)
- Corner-based pose proxy or planar homography
- Simple tracking (Kalman/IOU)
- Synthetic+real data fusion
- On-device latency targets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for distance and yaw from 4 corners
const wPix = bbox.width;
const f = fx;
const distance = (Wmm * f) / wPix;
const cx = bbox.centerX;
const dx = cx - cx0;
const yaw = Math.atan2(dx, f);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-planar barcodes or packaging with specular reflections?
- How would you tighten latency on a budget device?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests on-device few-shot adaptation, robust 3D pose in a cluttered retail environment, and strict latency constraints, plus ongoing data drift with weekly new items.

## Key Concepts

- On-device few-shot adaptation with prototypes
- 6-DoF pose from monocular RGB
- Temporal tracking with Kalman filter and data association
- Domain randomization for synthetic-real data
- Efficiency-focused backbone and head design

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-architecture sketch
class PoseHead {
  forward(featROI, box) {
    const phi = mlp(featROI, 128)
    const q = normalize(phi.slice(0,4))
    const t = phi.slice(4,7)
    return { q, t }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you disambiguate symmetric items (e.g., round cans) in pose estimation?
- How would you measure latency and ensure stable 50 ms/frame under thermal throttling?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge deployment, privacy-preserving CV, multi-camera fusion, and rapid adaptation to new attire in a security context.

## Key Concepts
- Edge inference, privacy-preserving anonymization, cross-camera re-ID, multi-camera tracking, 5-shot adaptation, latency.
- Synthetic+real data, losses for detection, embedding, privacy, and association.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch for a privacy-preserving forward pass
function forward(frame, model) {
  const det = model.detect(frame);
  const emb = model.embed(det.boxes);
  const blurred = blurFaces(frame, det.faces);
  return { blurred, det, emb };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you enforce privacy guarantees across jurisdictions?
- How would you measure cross-camera re-ID stability over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time non-rigid 3D reconstruction from monocular input, integrating differentiable rendering and on-device adaptation—critical for AR try-on. It also probes data strategy, evaluation, and edge-latency trade-offs in a commercial retail context.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular non-rigid 3D reconstruction with deformable garments
- Differentiable rendering and photometric consistency
- Temporal fusion for stability
- 5-shot on-device adaptation for unseen fabrics
- Edge latency and data strategy (synthetic+real)

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(frame, params){
  const latent = encoder(frame, params.enc);
  const mesh = deformableMesh(latent, params.shape);
  const render = renderer(mesh, params.tex);
  const loss = photometric(render, frame) + temporal(mesh);
  return {mesh, loss};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle occlusion or loose fabrics?
- How evaluate mesh quality vs. latency?
- How would you construct synthetic data for textiles?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design production-ready, on-device face verification systems with robust anti-spoofing capabilities under strict computational constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge-optimized inference architectures
- 3D head pose estimation from facial landmarks
- Temporal liveness detection for spoof resistance
- Parameter-efficient 5-shot adaptation via adapter layers
- Hybrid synthetic-to-real data generation strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: unified inference pipeline
function processFrame(frame) {
  const backbone = extractFeatures(frame);
  const detection = faceDetectionHead(backbone);
  const liveness = temporalLivenessHead(backbone);
  return { detection, liveness };
}
```
